> Actually, I think a less hacky way to do this would be to create a theme item variation in the modern/classic themes that checks if the current theme is light.

I tried other alternatives! But yeah, I'm very unfamiliar with contributing (this is my second PR), so I wouldn't be surprised if there's a better solution. Some of the things I checked out:

One of the issues I ran into is that _I think_ that the editor only generates icons once per theme change, and only a single variation at a time (from what I can gather from `editor_register_icons`). So I couldn't just use the regular unchecked icon for the 3D viewport. And adding an exception so that the icon isn't generated with a dim wouldn't work either, because that just makes the icon almost invisible everywhere else in a light theme.

As for putting the color in a theme variation, I guess it'd work obviously, but these checkboxes are _already_ under a theme variation: 
```c++
preview_camera->set_theme_type_variation("CheckBoxNoIconTint");
```
so I'd have to alter that one or make a copy but with the extra color. I really don't know if either of those is preferred over doing an override.

Changing the stylebox bg itself, to make it lighter in a light mode, seems to be no good because it's specifically left dark on all themes:
```c++
if (!p_config.dark_theme) {
	// Always use a dark background for the 3D viewport, even in light themes.
	// This is displayed as an overlay of the 3D scene, whose appearance doesn't change with the editor theme.
	// On top of that, dark overlays are more readable than light overlays.
	bg_color.invert();
}
```

And lastly, before committing this I did want to check if at least this hacky thing with the overbright colors has been used before, and it has! Found in `editor_audio_buses.cpp`:
```c++
Color solo_color = dark_icon_and_font ? Color(1.0, 0.89, 0.22) : Color(1.9, 1.74, 0.83);
Color mute_color = dark_icon_and_font ? Color(1.0, 0.16, 0.16) : Color(2.35, 1.03, 1.03);
Color bypass_color = dark_icon_and_font ? Color(0.13, 0.8, 1.0) : Color(1.03, 2.04, 2.35);
```
